\begin_layout Chapter
Verification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Unit Testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Initially, a directed testbench approach was employed during RTL development
 to inject instructions into the processor and observe its internal behavior.
 This allowed us to validate the pipeline stages, control flow, data forwarding,
 and exception handling in isolation before moving to full system-level
 testing.
 Behavioral logs and waveform analysis were critical during this phase,
 enabling detailed tracking of signal transitions, register file updates,
 memory transactions, and pipeline stalls, which facilitated quick debugging
 and refinement of the RTL.
 Following features were tested:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Verification of machine Mode privilege level and CSR instruction handling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Verification of Memory Management Unit (MMU) with Page table walker and
 TLB filling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
I-Cache and D-Cache memory transactions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
various internal exceptions such as load and store page faults, misaligned
 memory accesses, and illegal instructions.
 The core was observed for correct trap handling behavior, accurate CSR
 updates (like mcause and mtval), and recovery via mret.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
CSR testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A wide range of directed test cases were developed to target specific features
 and stress critical components of the processor.
 First, we verified the Machine Mode privilege level and CSR instruction
 handling, ensuring that privileged registers such as mstatus, mie, mepc,
 and mtvec responded correctly to reads and writes and enforced proper access
 controls.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Interrupt Testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The interrupt controller was tested using both asynchronous interrupt generation
 and timer interrupts, verifying correct transition into and out of trap
 handlers, proper CSR updates, and handling of nested or masked interrupts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
MMU Testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We also implemented and verified a complete Memory Management Unit (MMU),
 including two-level page table walking, address translation, and page access
 permission checks.
 These tests confirmed proper TLB filling, invalidation, and page fault
 generation based on access rights.
 In parallel, I-Cache and D-Cache behavior was evaluated through memory-intensiv
e programs to monitor cache hits, misses, replacements, and data coherence,
 ensuring correct integration with the memory system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Exception Testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To test the processor’s exception handling, we generated various internal
 exceptions such as load and store page faults, misaligned memory accesses,
 and illegal instructions.
 The core was observed for correct trap handling behavior, accurate CSR
 updates (like mcause and mtval), and recovery via mret.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
PMP Testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Additionally, PMP (Physical Memory Protection) functionality was verified
 by configuring regions using CSRs like pmpaddr and pmpcfg, and testing
 enforcement of access permissions across privilege boundaries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Debug Testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, a dedicated testbench environment was developed to verify the debug
 controller.
 We validated core halt and resume sequences using debug triggers like haltreq,
 tested single-step execution, and ensured the proper use of debug CSRs
 (dcsr, dpc, dscratch).
 Both abstract command execution and program buffer instruction handling
 were exercised to confirm full compliance with the RISC-V Debug Specification.
 Together, this verification strategy ensured a high level of confidence
 in the functional correctness and specification adherence of the processor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Formal Verification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once the individual functionalities of the processor were thoroughly verified
 through directed test cases, the next phase of verification focused on
 evaluating the processor’s behavior during full instruction execution.
 This involved testing how the core handles dynamic scenarios that arise
 during real program execution, such as data hazards, control hazards, and
 pipeline stalls caused by instruction dependencies or memory latency.
 These conditions cannot be fully captured through isolated unit-level tests,
 and therefore required system-level validation.
\end_layout

